#{[17592186230226 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's find the complementary solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n![](images/f0/f01ce3a6277c6af1c9c8eb4fc1b217b90b3ca72e33a727674c5a1670ccf51f5c.svg)\n\nTo find the complementary solution ![](images/12/12b17ac320da147720f235a1556bbb9bd404f3c8d100cf3b22d145a293d421d3.svg) we solve the corresponding homogeneous ODE.\nWe start by setting the right-hand side equal to zero:\n![](images/5b/5b189ffb1e490e04dc8aea566914128a88e9c48dcc331ff1f5a32ef4af6ee38c.svg)\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n![](images/a8/a8c8cee52474932eb90446a5d3007eea481549ca3be8de61aa32b0bead87e0e2.svg)\n\nWe separate the variables as follows:\n![](images/a0/a0cc584b2c78bd04361fa9ecc0a539f0466fcd22c9727965ff1b33318c542879.svg)\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to ![](images/06/06d9e6ee04f202e0e861c51f0cd11ce47727f565d64d834fa625baea04ae11e6.svg)\n![](images/b9/b962b410044f1e7be26c3bc15cded89ebc0d32a84d712e0f9fab3a953bc4ce5d.svg)\n\nNext, we make ![](images/02/024420a1f4a1300a242618c4742c03a91e27ee710937bb23acd902722711833c.svg) the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n![](images/ec/ec64cfca27de9b893156b962bcb27bd24af86fe03910cd89dbb48ae3c10d1e6d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/bc/bcee7e8a93dffc0dbf0dbd42566a68b0f25841376c48f286dc9670d635318d80.svg)\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the constant to be positive or negative (absorbing the sign into the constant).\nTherefore, the complementary solution is\n![](images/c2/c23ac0e71a5c90e57908d7220af6cc26641787e413a5cd2e1bec7a6a975f752d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230226 #edb/ref 1065 14698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230226 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "739946c6-4a73-5a29-a453-c3a79f65fe12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230226 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Complementary Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230236 #edb/ref 1064 "Technically, a particular solution $y_p$ is *not unique*.\n\nIf $y_p$ solves\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)\\,y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nand $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)\\,y = 0,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\ny_c + y_p\n$$\n\nalso solves the inhomogeneous equation, which means that $y_c + y_p$ is also a particular solution!\n\nHowever, in practice, even though $y_p$ is not unique, it’s very common to use the phrase **“the particular solution”** to mean \"a particular solution that *does not include* any terms from the complementary (homogeneous) solution\".\n\nSo, we typically choose $y_p$ to avoid overlapping with $y_c$, since the full solution is written as\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p.\n$$\n\nThis makes the roles of $y_c$ and $y_p$ clean and non-redundant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230236 #edb/ref 1065 14779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230236 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c8acee86-4e0b-5fcf-bd6d-8e401a83e24f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230236 #edb/ref 1067 "A Common Convention for “the” Particular Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230237 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the inhomogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 5 y = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nHere, the forcing term\n\n$$\nf(x) = 5x - 16\n$$\n\nis a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ so we guess\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nNow substitute $y_p$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha - 5(\\alpha x + \\beta ) = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nNext, group the terms proportional to $x$ and the constants:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x + (\\alpha - 5\\beta) = 5x-16.\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients on both sides gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -5\\beta =-16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-1$ and $\\beta=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  -x+3.\n$$\n\nLet's see another example where the forcing function is a quadratic polynomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230237 #edb/ref 1065 14798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230237 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40a1be07-f998-5d91-9693-ba72cd0662fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230237 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230238 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16.\n$$\n\nWe will find its general solution by computing the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using separation of variables gives\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-2/3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we will find a particular solution. To find a particular solution of\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16,\n$$\n\nnote that the forcing term is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nTherefore, we assume the particular solution is also a constant:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\beta.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ and its derivative into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 0 + 2\\beta = 16.\n$$\n\nSolving yields $\\beta = 8,$ so\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-2/3x} + 8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230238 #edb/ref 1065 14799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230238 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fd25308f-b077-5d29-98f3-6c1ad2c59cb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230238 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238697 #edb/ref 1080 "g:Z\\to Q" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238697 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238697 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49d64d53-9b2f-57ba-ae66-00d361f41b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238847 #edb/ref 1080 "g:Z\\to Z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238847 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238847 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b6b4e5c-e30d-5901-9e3c-d647641d6787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239341 #edb/ref 1080 "g:Q\\to Z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239341 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239341 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "94bd6867-a594-591a-a858-18374147272c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239710 #edb/ref 1080 "g:Z\\to R" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239710 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239710 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd9a8619-9664-5f90-b823-edd28ae0a5f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240026 #edb/ref 1080 "g:R\\to Z" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240026 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240026 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e0c5b799-4412-57c1-9c3e-9a1e5e7c754a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186238697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240620 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a35606cb-5bb9-5ad1-9e6b-622ff9614d62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240946 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $g$ maps the set of integers to the set of rational numbers.\"*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a267466-27f9-5907-b5d3-8a64993c6282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240946 #edb/ref 1074 "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of integers to the set of rational numbers\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $g:Z\\to Q$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $Z,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260021 #edb/ref 1080 "(r-⟨0,0,1⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,4,-4⟩=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260021 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260021 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f87268c-9742-5090-b7cb-521a5d8bbe66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261300 #edb/ref 1080 "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨-4,4,-2⟩=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261300 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261300 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a390ff13-0937-5eb3-b38b-3df5883b8889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261844 #edb/ref 1080 "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,-2,4⟩=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261844 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186261844 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "def43d7d-583f-5acb-a4e0-a37562571a5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262013 #edb/ref 1080 "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262013 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262013 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f4c13bd5-c05b-5f70-93b5-0ba177da222f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262119 #edb/ref 1080 "(r-⟨0,0,2⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,2⟩=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262119 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ffcc7a65-3